  "text": "```systemverilog\nmodule cdc_4phase #(\n  parameter WIDTH = 1,\n  parameter DECOUPLED = 1,\n  parameter SEND_RESET_MSG = 0,\n  parameter [WIDTH-1:0] RESET_MSG = {WIDTH{1'b0}}\n)(\n  input  src_rst_ni,\n  input  src_clk_i,\n  input  [WIDTH-1:0] src_data_i,\n  input  src_valid_i,\n  output src_ready_o,\n  input  dst_rst_ni,\n  input  dst_clk_i,\n  output [WIDTH-1:0] dst_data_o,\n  output dst_valid_o,\n  input  dst_ready_i\n);\n\n  // Source domain state\n  reg [WIDTH-1:0] src_data_reg;\n  reg             src_valid_reg;\n  reg             src_ready_reg;\n\n  // Destination domain state\n  reg [WIDTH-1:0] dst_data_reg;\n  reg             dst_valid_reg;\n  reg             dst_ready_reg;\n\n  // Synchronizers for valid signal (src -> dst)\n  reg [1:0] valid_sync;\n\n  // Synchronizers for ready signal (dst -> src)\n  reg [1:0] ready_sync;\n\n  // Synchronizers for data (src -> dst) - use a simple approach:\n  // We'll synchronize the data along with valid using a 2-stage synchronizer\n  // for each bit, but that's expensive. Instead, we'll use a different approach:\n  // We'll use a \"data valid\" handshake where we only change data when valid is low.\n\n  // Actually, for a 4-phase handshake, we need to be careful. Let's use a simpler\n  // approach: we'll use a 2-stage synchronizer for the valid signal, and we'll\n  // ensure data is stable when valid is asserted.\n\n  // Source domain: generate ready\n  // src_ready_o is high when we can accept new data\n  // We can accept new data when:\n  //   - We are not currently sending (src_valid_reg is low), OR\n  //   - DECOUPLED is set and the destination has acknowledged the previous transfer\n\n  // Let's use a state machine approach for the source side.\n  // States: IDLE, WAIT_ACK\n  // In IDLE: src_ready_o = 1, if src_valid_i, capture data, set src_valid_reg = 1, go to WAIT_ACK\n  // In WAIT_ACK: src_ready_o = 0 (or 1 if DECOUPLED and ack received), wait for ack from dst\n\n  // Actually, let's think about this more carefully. The 4-phase handshake means:\n  // 1. src_valid goes high (with data)\n  // 2. dst sees valid, processes, dst_ready goes high\n  // 3. src sees ready, src_valid goes low\n  // 4. dst sees valid low, dst_ready goes low\n\n  // For CDC, we need to synchronize these signals.\n\n  // Let's use a simpler approach:\n  // Source side:\n  //   - src_ready_o is high when we're not in the middle of a transfer\n  //   - When src_valid_i && src_ready_o, we capture data and assert src_valid_reg\n  //   - We deassert src_valid_reg when we see the synchronized ready from dst\n\n  // Destination side:\n  //   - dst_valid_o is the synchronized src_valid_reg\n  //   - dst_data_o is the synchronized data\n  //   - dst_ready_reg is asserted when dst_valid_o && dst_ready_i\n  //   - We deassert dst_ready_reg when we see the synchronized src_valid_reg go low\n\n  // Synchronizers\n  always @(posedge src_clk_i or negedge src_rst_ni) begin\n    if (!src_rst_ni) begin\n      valid_sync <= 2'b00;\n    end else begin\n      valid_sync <= {valid_sync[0], src_valid_reg};\n    end\n  end\n\n  always @(posedge dst_clk_i or negedge dst_rst_ni) begin\n    if (!dst_rst_ni) begin\n      ready_sync <= 2'b00;\n    end else begin\n      ready_sync <= {ready_sync[0], dst_ready_reg};\n    end\n  end\n\n  // Source domain logic\n  always @(posedge src_clk_i or negedge src_rst_ni) begin\n    if (!src_rst_ni) begin\n      src_data_reg  <= SEND_RESET_MSG ? RESET_MSG : {WIDTH{1'b0}};\n      src_valid_reg <= SEND_RESET_MSG ? 1'b1 : 1'b0;\n      src_ready_reg <= 1'b1;\n    end else begin\n      if (src_valid_reg) begin\n        // We are in the middle of a transfer\n        if (ready_sync[1]) begin\n          // Destination has acknowledged\n          src_valid_reg <= 1'b0;\n          if (DECOUPLED) begin\n            // Can accept new data immediately\n            if (src_valid_i) begin\n              src_data_reg  <= src_data_i;\n              src_valid_reg <= 1'b1;\n            end\n          end\n          src_ready_reg <= 1'b1;\n        end else begin\n          // Still waiting for ack\n          if (DECOUPLED && src_valid_i) begin\n            // In decoupled mode, we can accept new data even before ack\n            // But we need to be careful: we can only do this if we're not\n            // currently sending. Since src_valid_reg is high, we're sending.\n            // So we can't accept new data until we get the ack.\n            // Actually, DECOUPLED means we can pipeline: accept new data\n            // while the previous one is being acknowledged.\n            // But in a 4-phase handshake, we can't have two valids at once.\n            // So DECOUPLED might mean we can start preparing the next data.\n            // Let's keep it simple: don't accept new data until ack.\n          end\n          src_ready_reg <= 1'b0;\n        end\n      end else begin\n        // Not in a transfer\n        src_ready_reg <= 1'b1;\n        if (src_valid_i) begin\n          src_data_reg  <= src_data_i;\n          src_valid_reg <= 1'b1;\n        end\n      end\n    end\n  end\n\n  assign src_ready_o = src_ready_reg;\n\n  // Destination domain logic\n  // We need to synchronize the data as well. Since data changes when valid is low,\n  // we can use a 2-stage synchronizer for each bit, but that's expensive.\n  // Alternative: use a \"data latch\" that captures data when valid transitions.\n  \n  // Let's use a different approach: synchronize the data using a 2-stage\n  // synchronizer for each bit. This is safe because data is stable when\n  // valid is high.\n\n  // Actually, a simpler approach: use a 2-stage synchronizer for the data\n  // as well. Since data is stable when valid is high, this should work.\n\n  // But we need to be careful: the data might change while valid is low,\n  // and we don't want to capture that. So we should only update the\n  // synchronized data when valid is high.\n\n  // Let's use a \"data valid\" approach:\n  // - When valid_sync[1] is high, the data is stable and we can capture it.\n  // - We'll use a register that captures the data when valid is high.\n\n  // For simplicity, let's use a 2-stage synchronizer for each bit of data.\n  // This is the safest approach.\n\n  // Generate the synchronized data\n  reg [WIDTH-1:0] data_sync1;\n  reg [WIDTH-1:0] data_sync2;\n\n  always @(posedge dst_clk_i or negedge dst_rst_ni) begin\n    if (!dst_rst_ni) begin\n      data_sync1 <= {WIDTH{1'b0}};\n      data_sync2 <= {WIDTH{1'b0}};\n    end else begin\n      data_sync1 <= src_data_reg;\n      data_sync2 <= data_sync1;\n    end\n  end\n\n  // Destination valid and ready\n  always @(posedge dst_clk_i or negedge dst_rst_ni) begin\n    if (!dst_rst_ni) begin\n      dst_valid_reg <= SEND_RESET_MSG ? 1'b1 : 1'b0;\n      dst_ready_reg <= 1'b0;\n      dst_data_reg  <= SEND_RESET_MSG ? RESET_MSG : {WIDTH{1'b0}};\n    end else begin\n      if (dst_valid_reg) begin\n        // We have a valid item\n        if (dst_ready_i) begin\n          // Destination is ready, acknowledge\n          dst_ready_reg <= 1'b1;\n        end else begin\n          dst_ready_reg <= 1'b0;\n        end\n      end else begin\n        // No valid item\n        dst_ready_reg <= 1'b0;\n        if (valid_sync[1]) begin\n          // New valid from source\n          dst_valid_reg <= 1'b1;\n          dst_data_reg  <= data_sync2;\n        end\n      end\n    end\n  end\n\n  assign dst_valid_o = dst_valid_reg;\n  assign dst_data_o  = dst_data_reg;\n\nendmodule\n```<|im_end|>",
